```java
class Elevator {
    private final TreeSet<Integer> upStops = new TreeSet<>();
    private final TreeSet<Integer> downStops = new TreeSet<>();
    private Direction direction;
    private int currentFloor;

    void addRequest(int floor) {
        if (floor > currentFloor) upStops.add(floor);
        else if (floor < currentFloor) downStops.add(floor);
    }

    Integer nextStop() {
        if (direction == Direction.UP) {
            return !upStops.isEmpty() ? upStops.first()
                 : downStops.isEmpty() ? null : downStops.last();
        }
        return !downStops.isEmpty() ? downStops.last()
             : upStops.isEmpty() ? null : upStops.first();
    }
}
```

Two sorted sets, one per direction, are what make LOOK cheap: `nextStop()` always serves the
closest floor ahead in the current direction first, and only falls back to the other set —
meaning reverse — once the current direction's set is empty.

```java
class ElevatorController {
    private final ScoringStrategy scoring;

    void assignRequest(Request request) {
        Elevator best = null;
        int bestCost = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
        for (Elevator e : elevators) {
            int cost = scoring.score(e, request); // distance, direction match, load
            if (cost < bestCost) {
                bestCost = cost;
                best = e;
            }
        }
        best.addRequest(request.getFloor());
    }
}
```

`ScoringStrategy` is pluggable on purpose — swapping in a starvation-aware scorer or a
load-balancing scorer means writing a new class, not editing `assignRequest`'s dispatch loop.
